> # The Beast of Blackpine Village
> 
> A short investigation-and-rescue quest for:
> 
> - **Ekemon**, level 5 Fighter
> - **Varkash**, level 5 Fighter
> - **Dru**, level 4 Druid
> - **Oliver**, level 3 Rough
> 
> The beast is **Nightclaw**, a wounded **displacer beast**: a huge six-legged panther with black fur, glowing blue-violet eyes, and two barbed tentacles curling over its shoulders. Its magical displacement makes it appear to flicker several feet away from its real position.
> 
> Nightclaw is genuinely frightening—and potentially an excellent voluntary guardian for Ekemon’s Bastion—but it must be earned, not captured.
> 
> The real villains are a band of goblins who trapped Nightclaw while working for a mysterious buyer. They have dragged it into the abandoned **Moonfall Quarry** and intend to break it, sell it, or use it as a living weapon.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Opening Hook: The Druids’ Warning
> 
> Druids helping to establish Ekemon’s Bastion come with troubling news from the north.
> 
> > “The village of Blackpine has a beast problem. Goats have disappeared. Fences have been shredded. A hunter says he saw a black creature on a rooftop, but when he threw his spear, it passed through empty air.
> >
> > The villagers think it is a demon. The woods say it is wounded.”
> 
> They give Ekemon directions to Blackpine, roughly a day’s travel north through pine forest and rocky hills.
> 
> If Ekemon says he wants the creature for his Bastion, the oldest druid smiles carefully.
> 
> > “A beast with enough sense to fear a cage will never be a pet in the way you mean. But it may become a friend, if you make a place it wishes to defend.”
> 
> ---
> 
> # Blackpine Village
> 
> Blackpine is a northern village of roughly 150 people, built around goat herding, charcoal making, pinewood lumber, and a small quarry that was abandoned decades ago.
> 
> It sits beside **Moonwash Creek**, which flows northward through forest and eventually past the old **Moonfall Quarry**.
> 
> When the party arrives:
> 
> - livestock are shut indoors even in daylight;
> - villagers carry spears, wood axes, lanterns, and pitchforks;
> - children are being kept inside;
> - the village gate bears fresh painted words:
> 
> > **BEAST IN THE WOODS. KEEP CHILDREN INSIDE.**
> 
> The mayor, **Elira Fen**, is practical, exhausted, and not interested in taking chances.
> 
> > “We do not need a story. We need the attacks to stop.
> >
> > If you can make it leave alive, I will thank you. If you cannot, then do what must be done.”
> 
> She suggests several leads:
> 
> 1. **Farmer Tobin Rusk’s goat pen**, site of the latest attack.
> 2. **The Moonwash Creek trail**, where hunters found odd tracks.
> 3. **The Blackpine Taproom**, where frightened locals are spreading rumors.
> 4. **Old Warden Hessa’s cottage**, because she knows the abandoned quarry better than anyone.
> 5. Later, the party can investigate **Moonfall Quarry** itself.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Investigation Rules
> 
> The party should obtain around **three clear clues** before they confidently identify the quarry as the beast’s location and realize something else is involved.
> 
> Do not block progress behind failed rolls. A roll should determine extra detail, time saved, a useful advantage, or whether trouble finds them.
> 
> Suggested difficulties:
> 
> - **DC 11:** obvious evidence, friendly conversation
> - **DC 13:** normal tracking or investigation
> - **DC 15:** concealed details, difficult insight, or precise deduction
> 
> ---
> 
> # Location 1: The Broken Goat Pen
> 
> Farmer **Tobin Rusk** has lost two goats. A third survived but bears shallow cuts and is terrified of shadows.
> 
> Tobin is angry and has recruited three village hunters who plan to enter the woods tomorrow. They are badly equipped and likely to get themselves killed.
> 
> > “It was black as moonless night. Then it vanished. Then it was on my roof. I threw my spear straight at it—and the spear hit nothing!”
> 
> ### Clues
> 
> | Clue | Discovery | Meaning |
> |---|---|---|
> | Huge, six-toed feline tracks | Survival or Investigation DC 11 | The beast is catlike but much too large to be an ordinary animal. |
> | Gouges high on fence posts | Investigation DC 11 | The monster can climb or leap extraordinarily well; the marks may have been made by tentacles. |
> | Blue-violet iridescent blood | Investigation or Medicine DC 13; Dru can recognize its magical quality | The creature is wounded and bleeding. |
> | Frayed scraps of thick rope and weighted netting | Investigation DC 11 | Someone recently tried to capture or bind the creature. The materials are not local. |
> | A goat deliberately left alive | Insight DC 13 | The creature was feeding in desperation, not killing for sport. It fled before it could take more. |
> 
> ### Roleplaying opportunity
> 
> Tobin believes the beast must die. Ekemon can persuade, impress, or intimidate him into delaying the hunting party.
> 
> If Ekemon handles this well, Tobin provides:
> 
> - a **healing poultice** equivalent to one use of a healer’s kit;
> - a rough map of local animal trails;
> - two villagers who can guard Blackpine while the party investigates.
> 
> If Ekemon handles it poorly, Tobin and his hunters may later march toward the quarry, creating an additional rescue complication.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Location 2: The Blackpine Taproom
> 
> The taproom is full despite the fear. Everyone has a theory. Most are wrong.
> 
> Important NPCs:
> 
> - **Mira Venn**, a teenage stablehand who saw small figures near the creek.
> - **Sella Pike**, the innkeeper, who overheard suspicious travelers a few days ago.
> - **Bren Alder**, a drunk woodsman insisting the beast is a “forest devil.”
> - **Kip and Nessa**, two children who saw “little green men” carrying cages through the woods.
> 
> ### Rumors and clues
> 
> | Information | How to learn it | Truth |
> |---|---|---|
> | “The beast has glowing eyes and lives in the quarry.” | General rumor | Mostly true, but it was only recently taken there. |
> | “Little green people were seen near the creek.” | Speak with Mira or the children; DC 11 | True: goblins were scouting and setting traps. |
> | “Three cloaked traders bought iron chains from the smith.” | Speak with Sella; DC 13 | True: the goblins had help or a buyer nearby. |
> | “The beast ate moonberries by the creek.” | Speak with Bren after gaining his trust, or DC 13 Insight | True. It was searching for medicinal plants. |
> | “The quarry is haunted.” | General rumor | Not literally, but goblin torchlight and Nightclaw’s displacement magic produce ghostly effects. |
> 
> ### Optional social challenge: The frightened crowd
> 
> While the party is here, a villager rushes in shouting that shadows are moving around the northern goat fields.
> 
> This is not Nightclaw. It is **two goblin scouts** trying to steal food while the village is distracted.
> 
> The party can pursue them, leading directly to the first battle.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Battle 1: Goblin Scouts at the Pine Fields
> 
> Use:
> 
> - **4 Goblins**
> - **1 Goblin Boss**
> 
> This should be a brisk, satisfying battle for the party. The goblins are not trying to fight to the death: they are stealing provisions, spying on the village, and trying to make sure nobody follows the quarry trail.
> 
> ### Terrain
> 
> The field contains:
> 
> - low stone walls granting half cover;
> - stacks of firewood;
> - panicked goats;
> - a muddy drainage ditch;
> - one rickety hayloft or barn roof for goblin archers.
> 
> ### Goblin behavior
> 
> - Goblins fire from cover and retreat.
> - The boss shouts commands and tries to flee if reduced below half hit points.
> - A fleeing goblin carries a crude charcoal map showing **Moonwash Creek** and a skull-like mark at **Moonfall Quarry**.
> - If captured, the boss reveals:
> 
> > “We got the shadow-cat! Big Boss Grik says it’ll fetch a mountain of gold! Maybe the Bone-Merchant buys it. Maybe he feeds it to us first!”
> 
> The “Bone-Merchant” can be a future villain or merely a name the goblins use to sound important.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Location 3: Moonwash Creek Trail
> 
> Following the creek north takes the party into denser forest. There are areas of mud, rocky outcroppings, thorny brush, and old pines so tall that the forest seems to swallow daylight.
> 
> This is where the party can establish that Nightclaw was ambushed and captured.
> 
> ### Clues
> 
> | Clue | Discovery | Meaning |
> |---|---|---|
> | Tough black hairs caught in bark | Survival or Investigation DC 11 | The creature passed through here. Its pelt is unusually dense and magical. |
> | Crushed moonberries and medicinal root plants | Dru automatically notices, or Survival DC 13 | The beast was seeking plants to soothe injury or poison. |
> | Broken branches and churned mud | Survival DC 13 | There was a struggle involving a large creature and several small humanoids. |
> | Goblin footprints, mixed with booted human-sized tracks | Survival DC 15 | Goblins were assisted by one or two larger figures—possibly mercenaries or handlers. |
> | A broken weighted net with iron hooks | Investigation DC 13 | The net was built to restrain something powerful. Its ropes match scraps at the goat pen. |
> | More blue-violet blood, now mixed with ash | Medicine DC 13 | The creature was burned or shocked by a magical restraint, then dragged north. |
> 
> ### Dru’s moment
> 
> Dru can speak with local animals or use nature magic.
> 
> A raven saw:
> 
> > “Little knife-men. Fire rope. Shadow-cat screamed. They took it where stone mouths drink the creek.”
> 
> A fox reports:
> 
> > “The black one did not hunt us. It ran. It hurt. The small ones followed.”
> 
> This makes Nightclaw’s situation unmistakably sympathetic without making it tame.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Battle 2: The Creek Ambush
> 
> As the party follows the trail, goblins attempt to stop them before they reach the quarry.
> 
> Use:
> 
> - **5 Goblins**
> - **2 Wolves**, trained and half-starved
> - Optional: **1 Goblin Boss** if the first boss escaped or the party easily defeated the scouts.
> 
> This is a tactical woodland battle. It is not intended to be a slaughter; the goblins want delay, injury, and retreat.
> 
> ### Terrain features
> 
> - A narrow creek crossing over slick rocks.
> - A fallen pine trunk forming a bridge.
> - Thorny brambles that count as difficult terrain.
> - A steep, muddy bank.
> - A concealed snare trap.
> 
> ### Snare trap
> 
> A character who enters a marked square or fails a DC 13 Perception check triggers a rope snare.
> 
> - **Dexterity save DC 13**
> - On a failure, the creature is hoisted upside down 10 feet into the air and restrained.
> - Escaping requires an action and DC 13 Athletics, Acrobatics, or a cutting weapon.
> 
> Varkash can have a great heroic moment cutting someone down while under fire. Oliver can spot and disable the trap. Dru can reshape the battlefield with plants or terrain magic.
> 
