> # Location 4: Old Warden Hessa’s Cottage
> 
> **Old Warden Hessa** is a retired quarry worker, former village guard, and one of the few people alive who knows Moonfall Quarry’s old tunnels.
> 
> Her tidy little stone cottage stands at the forest’s edge, nearer the quarry than any sensible person would choose to live. She is sharp-eyed, blunt, and not impressed by adventurers.
> 
> > “The quarry ain’t haunted. It’s worse than haunted. It’s unstable, it’s full of holes, and now it’s got goblins with enough confidence to use both.”
> 
> Hessa initially assumes the party has come to kill the monster. If Ekemon explains that he wants to rescue it—or at least understand what happened—she becomes more cooperative.
> 
> > “Good. Killing a wounded animal is easy. Getting one out alive, with goblins shooting at you, now that’s a job.”
> 
> ## What Hessa knows
> 
> | Information | How to learn it | Truth |
> |---|---|---|
> | Several old quarry tunnels connect to the main pit. | Conversation or DC 11 Persuasion | True, though some are collapsed. |
> | There is a hidden side entrance through a narrow fissure. | Gain Hessa’s trust; or DC 13 Investigation around her old maps | True. It bypasses the main gate. |
> | The quarry has a deep central shaft and old lifting gear. | Conversation | True. Nightclaw is imprisoned there. |
> | A lower tunnel meets Moonwash Creek. | DC 11 Survival, or Hessa’s map | True. It is damp, narrow, and lightly guarded. |
> | The southern quarry wall is unstable. | DC 13 Insight or investigation of the map | True. A deliberate rockslide could block a tunnel or create a diversion. |
> | Goblins have been in the area for about a week. | Conversation | True. They arrived before the attacks on the village. |
> 
> ## Hessa’s aid
> 
> If the party has treated villagers well, shown her the weighted net, or convinced her that Nightclaw is a captive rather than a marauder, Hessa provides:
> 
> - a hand-drawn **quarry map**;
> - directions to the **hidden side entrance**;
> - a small pouch of blasting powder, enough to collapse a fragile support or cause a loud diversion;
> - one **healing potion** she has saved “for when I get foolish enough to need it.”
> 
> She also issues the important warning:
> 
> > “If you find the beast in chains, don’t crowd it. A scared cat will take your hand off. A scared *shadow-cat* may take your hand off from somewhere else entirely.”
> 
> ---
> 
> # Moonfall Quarry
> 
> Moonfall Quarry is an abandoned open-pit mine about a mile north of Blackpine. Pines and thorny brush have reclaimed much of the site, but the old quarry remains an ugly wound in the hillside.
> 
> Broken cranes, rotted scaffolds, rusted ore carts, and piles of pale stone surround a broad, descending pit. Dark tunnels cut into the quarry walls. Moonwash Creek passes along its northern edge and enters the rock through a low, partially flooded tunnel.
> 
> At night, goblin fires flicker between the rocks. Their light, reflected by Nightclaw’s uncontrolled displacement magic, makes the quarry look genuinely haunted.
> 
> ## Quarry approach options
> 
> ### 1. Main entrance
> 
> A broad, obvious cut into the stone.
> 
> - Best for a direct assault.
> - Guarded by **2 goblins** in elevated positions.
> - Trapped with a crude rockfall and an alarm line.
> - The rockfall can be spotted with **DC 13 Perception** or disabled with **DC 13 Thieves’ Tools / Investigation**.
> 
> If triggered, creatures in the entryway make a **DC 13 Dexterity save**, taking modest bludgeoning damage and becoming temporarily blocked by loose stone on a failure.
> 
> ### 2. Hidden side fissure
> 
> A narrow, overgrown crack in the southern quarry wall, marked on Hessa’s map.
> 
> - Requires squeezing through tight stone for a short distance.
> - Leads into an old storage tunnel near the main pit.
> - Guarded only by one inattentive goblin, unless the alarm has been raised.
> - Ideal for Oliver to scout ahead.
> 
> ### 3. Moonwash Creek tunnel
> 
> A low tunnel where creek water flows beneath the quarry.
> 
> - Dru can use this route well, especially with water or animal magic.
> - It is difficult terrain and partially submerged.
> - Two goblins patrol it from a ledge above.
> - It leads close to the lower end of the deep shaft.
> 
> ### 4. Climbing or descending the quarry wall
> 
> The party can approach from above and descend into the pit.
> 
> - **DC 14 Athletics** without appropriate gear.
> - A fall is not likely to kill a level 5 fighter, but it can be painful and noisy.
> - This route lets the party see the central shaft and several guard positions from above.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Quarry Defenses
> 
> The quarry is a small dungeon rather than a single battle. Goblins know the ground and want to use it.
> 
> ## Lookouts
> 
> Goblins occupy high ledges and broken crane platforms. They carry bows and bone whistles.
> 
> If warned, they do not fight to the death. They shoot, retreat, and call more goblins into better positions.
> 
> ## Traps
> 
> Use two or three of these, depending on how much dungeon play you want.
> 
> ### Tripwire and swinging logs
> 
> A narrow passage contains a wire hidden beneath leaf litter.
> 
> - **DC 13 Perception** to spot.
> - **DC 13 Dexterity save** when triggered.
> - On a failure, take bludgeoning damage and be knocked prone.
> - On a success, take half damage and remain standing.
> 
> ### Loose scree slope
> 
> A steep descent into the pit is covered with loose stones.
> 
> - **DC 12 Acrobatics or Athletics** to descend safely at speed.
> - On a failure, slide 15 feet, fall prone, and potentially alert a nearby patrol.
> 
> ### Alarm cans
> 
> Tin cups, quarry tools, and bits of bone are tied to a line across a tunnel.
> 
> - **DC 11 Perception** to notice.
> - Easily bypassed or cut once found.
> - If triggered, all goblins in the quarry become alert.
> 
> ## Patrol
> 
> A standard patrol consists of:
> 
> - **3 Goblins**
> - optionally **1 wolf**
> 
> The goblins carry supplies: raw meat, iron chains, lamp oil, and rough bandages. The meat is intended for Nightclaw, though the goblins usually throw it down the shaft while laughing at it.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Encounter: The Goblin Work Crew
> 
> Before reaching the shaft, the party can encounter goblins working in an old storage cavern.
> 
> Use:
> 
> - **4 Goblins**
> - **1 Goblin Boss**
> - Optional: a crude barricade granting half cover
> 
> The crew is preparing a wagon cage for transport. The cage is too small for Nightclaw to be comfortable; it is built to keep it broken, chained, and unable to use its tentacles.
> 
> A successful search reveals:
> 
> - a shipping mark: a skull surrounded by a ring of small bones;
> - a partial contract written in poor Common:
> 
> > **“One live shadow-cat. Payment upon delivery. Do not damage hide. Keep collar active.”**
> 
> - a small black-metal charm used as part of Nightclaw’s magical restraint.
> 
> This establishes the larger mystery: someone called the **Bone-Merchant** is buying magical beasts.
> 
> ---
> 
> # The Deep Shaft: Nightclaw’s Prison
> 
> At the quarry’s center lies a vertical shaft, thirty feet deep and perhaps forty feet across. Old wooden scaffolding surrounds its rim. A rusty winch once used to haul stone now holds heavy chains and a cargo platform.
> 
> At the bottom, amid broken rock and shallow water, is Nightclaw.
> 
> It is unmistakable: a huge, black, six-legged feline with shoulder tentacles, wounds across its flanks, and blue-violet eyes burning in the dark. Its image flickers and doubles unpredictably. Sometimes it appears at the shaft’s far wall; sometimes it seems to loom directly beneath whoever looks down.
> 
> But its real body is pinned by:
> 
> - thick iron chains around its forelegs and torso;
> - a cruel barbed collar;
> - a black-metal suppression charm mounted on the wall;
> - a net of chain weighted with quarry stones.
> 
> It has around half its normal health and is exhausted, hungry, and terrified.
> 
> Two goblins at the rim jab spears downward whenever it moves.
> 
> > “Stay still, shadow-cat! Big Boss says the buyer likes the hide unmarked!”
> 
> If the party attacks openly, Nightclaw initially lashes out at anyone who descends. It cannot distinguish rescuers from captors until the goblins and restraints are dealt with.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Freeing Nightclaw
> 
> This should be a multi-part rescue rather than a single check.
> 
> ## Step 1: Stop the tormentors
> 
> Defeat, scare off, or otherwise neutralize the guards.
> 
> Use:
> 
> - **2 Goblins** at the shaft rim
> - **1 Goblin Boss**, supervising the winch
> 
> If the quarry alarm was raised, add 2 more goblins.
> 
> ## Step 2: Reach the bottom
> 
> Options include:
> 
> - lower someone on the cargo platform;
> - climb down the old scaffolding;
> - use rope and grappling equipment;
> - enter through the creek tunnel, which reaches a lower side opening;
> - employ magic or clever construction.
> 
> The winch is old.
> 
> - **DC 12 Investigation** identifies its weak points.
> - **DC 13 Strength** operates it safely under pressure.
> - On a bad failure, it jams or breaks—but the party can still use rope, magic, or the scaffolding.
> 
> ## Step 3: Disable the suppression charm
> 
> The black-metal charm is the core of the restraint. It prevents Nightclaw from using its displacement ability properly and causes continual pain.
> 
> It can be handled in several ways:
> 
> - **Arcana or Religion DC 14:** identify its function and safely disconnect it.
> - **Thieves’ Tools / Sleight of Hand DC 14:** remove it from its bracket.
> - **Attack it:** AC around 15, modest hit points, resistant to nonmagical damage if desired.
> - **Dispel Magic or comparable magic:** automatically works if appropriate to your system.
> - **Dru’s magic:** Dru may identify it as a corrupted binding charm and weaken it through a nature-based ritual.
> 
> Destroying it causes a burst of shadowy blue force. Everyone within a short distance makes a moderate Dexterity save or takes minor psychic/force damage. Nightclaw’s displacement ability returns immediately, though it remains chained.
> 
> ## Step 4: Remove the chains
> 
> The chains can be:
> 
> - picked with **DC 17 Thieves’ Tools**;
> - broken with **DC 17 Athletics**;
> - cut with appropriate tools, magic, or repeated weapon attacks;
> - opened using keys carried by Big Boss Grik.
> 
> Ekemon or Varkash may be able to accomplish this by sheer strength, especially with leverage from a crowbar, winch, or broken quarry tool.
> 
> ## Step 5: Demonstrate that the party is not a new cage
> 
> Nightclaw’s trust is earned by what the party does.
> 
> The strongest actions are:
> 
> - healing it;
> - ending the pain of the collar and chains;
> - protecting it from goblins
